":0.95,"doc":"AUTOSAR_AP_SWS_Cryptography","id":"SNIP_crypto_AUTOSAR_AP_SWS_C_P28_94","is_figure":false,"page":28,"relation":"API_IMPLEMENTATION","targets":["SWS_CRYPT_30403"],"text":"[SWS_CRYPT_00005] [SWS_CRYPT_00006] [SWS_CRYPT_00009] [SWS_CRYPT_10017] [SWS_CRYPT_20098] [SWS_CRYPT_20099] [SWS_CRYPT_20654] [SWS_CRYPT_20700] [SWS_CRYPT_30001] [SWS_CRYPT_30002] [SWS_CRYPT_30003] [SWS_CRYPT_30099] [SWS_CRYPT_30100] [SWS_CRYPT_30130] [SWS_CRYPT_30131] [SWS_CRYPT_30403] [SWS_CRYPT_40911]","why":[{"c":0.95,"m":"cached_context_sweep/agy","mod":"gemini-3.8-flash","r":"Spezifikations-Zitat SWS_CRYPT_30403 im Belegtext.","rel":"API_IMPLEMENTATION","rls":["R19-11","R20-11","R21-11","R22-11","R23-11","R24-11","R25-11"],"role":"constitutive","t":["SWS_CRYPT_30403"]}]},{"confidence":0.85,"doc":"AUTOSAR_AP_SWS_Cryptography","id":"FIG-29c1d438b89bc459","is_figure":true,"page":36,"relation":"heuristic","targets":["SWS_CRYPT_30404"],"text":"Figure 7.3: Concurrent access to a single Key Slot","why":[{"c":0.85,"m":"cached_context_sweep/agy","mod":"gemini-3.8-flash","r":"Register update observer callback on key slot","role":"heuristic","t":["SWS_CRYPT_30404"]}]},{"confidence":0.85,"doc":"AUTOSAR_AP_EXP_SWArchitecture","id":"FIG-899dfee54f9ba78b","is_figure":true,"page":113,"relation":"heuristic","targets":["SWS_CRYPT_30404"],"text":"Figure 9.65: Cryptographic Key Handling Interfaces","why":[{"c":0.85,"m":"cached_context_sweep/agy","mod":"gemini-3.8-flash","r":"Registering UpdateObserver callback on KeySlot","role":"heuristic","t":["SWS_CRYPT_30404"]}]},{"confidence":0.8,"doc":"SWS_Cryptography","id":"SNIP_ADAPTIVE_CRYPTO_FIGURE_Figure_7_1","is_figure":true,"page":39,"relation":"heuristic","targets":["SWS_CRYPT_30403"],"text":"Figure 7.1: Exemplary High-level FC Crypto architecture A CryptoProvider provides access to the implementation of a set of cryptographic prim- itives and a key storage. T ypically , an cryptographic module within a trusted execution environment is providing the actual implementation of primitives and key storage, and the CryptoProvider is providing a wrapper that allows access to different implementa- tions using the same interface. A CryptoProvider is implemented by a stack vendor or a third party by implementing the ara::crypto::cryp::CryptoProvider class, as well as the classes for cryptographic contexts and the classes for cryptographic keys. It is possible to have multiple different CryptoProviders instantiated on one ma- chine. However, although the interfaces are the same across multiple CryptoProvider implementations, there is no interoperability between different CryptoProviders. For instance, it is not possible to use a symmetric key that belongs to one CryptoProvider for encryption in another CryptoProvider. This is not a limitation of ara::crypto but an inherent and desired property of cryptographic modules. Every key managed by ara::crypto is managed by exactly one CryptoProvider. A ara:: crypto::cryp::KeySlot represents the interface from the application to access and use a persisted key . Each KeySlot is statically mapped to one CryptoProvider during deployment. Access to KeySlots is provided to applications through modelled ports using InstanceSpecifiers. The X.509 Provider provides access to persisted certificates and supports certificate management and verification functionality . In contrast to the CryptoProvider, there exists exactly one X.509 Provider instance on one machine.","why":[{"c":0.8,"m":"cached_context_sweep/agy","mod":"gemini-3.8-flash","r":"KeySlot mapped to specific CryptoProvider","role":"heuristic","t":["SWS_CRYPT_30403"]}]},{"confidence":0.8,"doc":"AUTOSAR_AP_SWS_Cryptography","id":"SNIP_crypto_AUTOSAR_AP_SWS_C_P231_1254","is_figure":false,"page":231,"relation":"heuristic","targets":["SWS_CRYPT_50146"],"text":"Specification of Cryptography AUTOSAR AP R23-11 [SWS_CRYPT_30401] {DRAFT} Definition of API function ara::crypto::keys::Key Slot::~KeySlot ⌈","why":[{"c":0.8,"m":"cached_context_sw